-- title:
--   Equivariant chains rule out finite cyclic subgroups
-- statement:
--   Let $G$ be a group and $H\le G$ a finite cyclic subgroup generated by $g$. Consider free chain modules $C_i=\mathbb Z[A_i\times G]$ with $H$-equivariant maps $\partial_1:C_1\to C_0$ and $\partial_2:C_2\to C_1$. Assume $\partial_1\partial_2=0$ and the augmentation $\varepsilon$ vanishes on $\partial_1$. Suppose there are $v\in C_0$ with $\varepsilon(v)=1$, $c\in C_1$ and a $g$-invariant $D\in C_2$ with
--   $$
--   \partial_1c=g\cdot v-v,\qquad \partial_2D=\sum_{h\in H}h\cdot c .
--   $$
--   Then $g=1$.
--
--   Applied to the cellular chains of the Cayley complex, this shows that the group has no nontrivial elements of finite order.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/OAI/GroupTheory/Hyperbolic/CyclicAction.lean#L192-L223

